\section{Methods}

\subsection{Hardware Configuration}
UPAS-X was executed on a 16-node cluster equipped with 128 NVIDIA H100 GPUs 
(80GB HBM3 each), dual AMD EPYC 9654 CPUs per node, and 1.5 TB DDR5-4800 RAM. 
Nodes were interconnected via NVSwitch, NVLink 4.0, and HDR InfiniBand (400 Gb/s).

\subsection{Ephemeris Integration}
Orbital trajectories were computed using a 15th-order Runge--Kutta--Nyström 
symplectic integrator with adaptive step-size control. Hamiltonian drift was 
monitored continuously:
\[
\left| \frac{H(t)-H(0)}{H(0)} \right| < 10^{-10}.
\]

\subsection{BVH Construction and Traversal}
A Surface Area Heuristic (SAH) binned builder generated the BVH hierarchy. 
Dynamic motion triggered GPU-based refit kernels. BVH traversal depth and 
rebuild frequency were logged for stability analysis.

\subsection{Ray--Cone Intersection}
Umbra, penumbra, and antumbra boundaries were computed using analytic cone 
geometry. CUDA kernels evaluated intersection distances and flux attenuation 
for each ray.

\subsection{Flux Modeling}
Stellar flux fields were generated using Gaia DR3 and Kepler limb-darkening 
coefficients. HDR flux linearity was validated across shadow zones.

\subsection{Validation Datasets}
\begin{itemize}
    \item NASA GSFC eclipse maps (2017-08-21, 2024-04-08)
    \item NASA JPL HORIZONS ephemerides (DE440/DE441)
    \item Gaia DR3 / Kepler photometric catalogs
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Performance Measurement}
Rays/sec throughput, frame latency, VRAM utilization, and BVH traversal depth 
were recorded under increasing spatial node counts and source densities.

\subsection{Reproducibility}
Deterministic execution was enforced using SHA-256 seed locking, immutable JSON 
configuration manifests, and semantic versioning across all simulation campaigns.